{"id":"53ef0088-426d-4d08-8018-0491b4c188af","object":"chat.completion","created":1784886452,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    distances = case['distances']\\n    n = len(nodes)\\n    # Build edges with mutual reachability\\n    edges = []\\n    for u, v, d in distances:\\n        mr = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        # Canonicalize endpoints: sort u, v lexicographically for tie-breaking\\n        if u < v:\\n            edges.append((mr, u, v))\\n        else:\\n            edges.append((mr, v, u))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda e: (e[0], e[1], e[2]))\\n    # Union-Find\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for mr, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, mr])\\n            total_weight += mr\\n            if len(mst_edges) == n - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != n - 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected, cannot compute MST\\\")\\n    return {\\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that computes MST using mutual reachability distances. The input case contains nodes (list of node IDs), core_distance (dict mapping node->float), distances (list of [node1, node2, distance]). For each pair, compute mutual reachability = max(core[node1], core[node2], distance). Then sort edges by (reachability, node1, node2) canonical order. Use Kruskal with union-find. Return {\"mst_edges\": list of [u, v, weight], \"total_weight\": sum}. Note: The output schema requires each edge to be a 3-element array: [string, string, number]. Must ensure endpoints canonicalized (maybe sorted lexicographically? but specification says canonicalize undirected endpoints -> probably sort by node IDs). Ensure deterministic tie-breaking: for equal reachability, sort by node IDs (maybe first node, then second). Must handle disconnected? Problem says \"Reject disconnected inputs\" - but not clear what to do if graph is disconnected? Probably assume connected, but if not, raise? But output must return mst_edges for spanning tree? Actually the algorithm should select n-1 edges; if graph is disconnected, Kruskal will not be able to select n-1 edges, so we need to detect and maybe raise exception or return empty? But instruction says \"Reject disconnected inputs\". Probably the test cases are all connected. We'll implement: if number of selected edges != len(nodes)-1, raise ValueError. But the function must return JSON-serializable object; we could return something but best to assume connected. We'll implement check and raise Exception which will cause failure, but maybe better to handle gracefully? However spec says \"Reject disconnected inputs\" so raising is acceptable. We'll just assume connectivity.\n\nImplementation details: Use union-find class. For each edge in distances, compute mr = max(core_distance[u], core_distance[v], dist). Build list of (mr, u, v). Then sort by (mr, u, v). For each, if find(u) != find(v), union, add to mst, accumulate weight. After loop, check len(mst)==n-1, else raise. Return mst_edges (with canonical order maybe u<v? We'll keep from sorted order which ensures canonical because we sort by u then v). Done.\n\nNote: the key _case_nonce is present but not used. We only use fields in payload.\n\nImplementation code as Python source string. Must be within JSON object with key \"implementation\".\n\nOutput should be exactly the JSON string containing the Python function.\n\nWrite solve(case) and return the result.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer as JSON with implementation key."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1906,"completion_tokens":1030,"total_tokens":2936,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":566},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":114},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}